<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philipp Albert Stapfer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philipp Albert Stapfer was a Swiss theologian and statesman who served as minister of arts and sciences of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> from 1798. He planned a national system of public schools, a national university and a national library, and supported the educator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</a>. He later represented the republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philipp-albert-stapfer.jpg" alt="Philipp Albert Stapfer" width="200" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploaded by Voyager (Transferred by Terfili) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philipp_Albert_Stapfer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philipp Albert Stapfer was born in Bern on 23 September 1766. He studied at the Georg August University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> in 1789 and 1790, where Friedrich Bouterwek, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>, was already lecturing on Kant's philosophy. Stapfer followed the development of Kant's work through Bouterwek for the rest of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As Minister of Sciences and Arts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> in 1798, Stapfer arranged for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</a> to be named chief editor of the government paper Helvetisches Volksblatt, a post Pestalozzi soon resigned. From 1801 to 1803, the years leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a>, Stapfer was the plenipotentiary envoy of the Helvetic Republic to the French Consulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. There he pleaded Pestalozzi's case for educational reform with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, who dismissed the programme for its alleged lack of real science.</p><p>Stapfer married and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the Château de Talcy in Loir-et-Cher and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In 1805 he became a friend of the philosopher Maine de Biran at the informal gatherings of the Cabanis circle at Auteuil, and the two shared an interest in Pestalozzi's methods; Maine de Biran later invited a teacher trained by Pestalozzi at Yverdon to Bergerac. Stapfer was also vice-president of the Paris Protestant society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>After Maine de Biran settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1812, Stapfer took part in the philosophical debates he organised, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-marie-ampere">André-Marie Ampère</a>, Joseph-Marie de Gérando, Pierre Paul Royer-Collard and Georges Cuvier. With de Gérando he was one of Maine de Biran's main sources on Kant. Maine de Biran addressed to him the essay of 1818 known as the Réponses à Stapfer, and wrote at his instigation an exposition of the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> for the Biographie Universelle, published in 1819 with Stapfer's long notes.</p><p>Stapfer outlived both men. He held a salon of his own, where guests such as Victor Cousin, Sainte-Beuve and Guizot heard him as a witness of the preceding decades, and he urged Cousin and Joseph Lainé to classify and publish Maine de Biran's works. His own writings include two essays on reading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, of 1821 and 1823, and the Mélanges philosophiques, littéraires, historiques et religieux, published in 1844. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 27 March 1840.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philipp Albert Stapfer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Minister <b>Philipp Albert Stapfer</b> planned public schooling.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in former 3rd arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1814" aria-label="Bern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4725 515 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philipp Albert Stapfer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5175 785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1814">What was here in 1814? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
